  "question": {
    "text": "To raise the temperature of a certain mass of gas by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> at a constant pressure,<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>160</mn></math>&nbsp;calories of heat is required. When the same mass of gas is cooled by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> at constant volume,<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>240</mn></math> calories of heat is released. How many degrees of freedom does each molecule of this gas have (assume gas to be ideal)?",
